---
// Privacy Policy (SITE-STRUCTURE.md page 17). The text is strale-frontend's
// src/pages/Privacy.tsx, word for word: it is a legal notice of Moonlighter AB, so any change
// to it is Petter's. As before, the two retention periods are read, not typed:
//   audit-record retention        facts.static.retention_days_default
//   longest configurable period   facts.static.retention_days_max_configurable
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import LegalDoc from "../components/LegalDoc.astro";
import { getSiteData } from "../lib/site-data";
import { formatPeriod } from "../lib/audit-record";

const LAST_UPDATED = "5 November 2026";
const CONTACT_EMAIL = "compliance@strale.io";

const facts = (await getSiteData()).facts.static;
const maxDays = facts.retention_days_max_configurable;
if (typeof maxDays !== "number" || !Number.isFinite(maxDays)) {
  throw new Error("apps/web/privacy: platform facts carry no static.retention_days_max_configurable; the Privacy Policy states it, so the build stops rather than guess");
}
const defaultPeriod = formatPeriod(facts.retention_days_default);
const maxPeriod = formatPeriod(maxDays);
---
<Base title="Privacy Policy" description="How Strale collects, uses, and protects personal data. GDPR and UK GDPR compliant.">
  <LegalDoc title="Privacy Policy" updated={LAST_UPDATED}>
    <p slot="intro">
      This policy explains how Strale collects, uses, and protects
      personal data. Questions:{" "}
      <a class="link" href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>.
    </p>

    <section id="controller">
      <h2>1. Who we are</h2>
      <p>
        The Service is operated by <strong>Moonlighter AB</strong> (Sweden,
        organisationsnummer 559395-7979). For data-protection enquiries,
        contact{" "}
        <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Moonlighter AB is the data controller for account, billing, and usage
        data described below. For data that customers send to the API to be
        processed by a capability (e.g. counterparty information for Payee
        Assurance), Moonlighter AB acts as a data processor on the customer&rsquo;s
        behalf. A data processing agreement (DPA) is available on request.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="data">
      <h2>2. Data we process</h2>
      <p>We process three categories of personal data:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Account &amp; billing:</strong> name, email, organisation,
          hashed API keys, billing records. Card and bank details are handled
          by our payment processor — we do not see or store them. On-chain
          wallet addresses used for x402 payments are public by design.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Usage:</strong> request metadata (timestamp, IP, capability,
          response status, latency), audit records (input fingerprint as
          SHA-256 hash, output schema validation result, transparency
          markers), and minimal cookieless website analytics.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Customer-input:</strong> data customers send to the API for
          processing by a capability. May include personal data (e.g. names,
          VAT numbers, beneficial-ownership data) and, for some capabilities,
          special-category data under Art. 9 GDPR (PEP / sanctions screening)
          or criminal-conviction data under Art. 10 GDPR (adverse-media
          checks). We process this data only to execute the requested
          capability. We do not use it to train models and do not sell it.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section id="purposes">
      <h2>3. Why we process it (lawful basis)</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Contract (Art. 6(1)(b) GDPR):</strong> to provide the
          Service — issuing API keys, executing API calls, generating audit
          records, billing.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Legitimate interests (Art. 6(1)(f) GDPR):</strong> security,
          fraud and abuse prevention, rate-limiting, audit-trail integrity,
          and aggregate product improvement.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Legal obligation (Art. 6(1)(c) GDPR):</strong> accounting,
          tax, anti-money-laundering record-keeping, and lawful regulator
          requests.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Consent (Art. 6(1)(a) GDPR):</strong> for marketing emails,
          withdrawable at any time.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Where customers use capabilities involving Art. 9 / Art. 10 data, the
        customer is responsible for ensuring an appropriate condition under
        Art. 9 / Art. 10 (typically substantial public interest, fraud
        prevention, or comparable lawful basis).
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="recipients">
      <h2>4. Recipients</h2>
      <p>
        We share personal data only with categories of recipients necessary
        to operate the Service: cloud hosting and database providers, payment
        processors, AI inference providers (for AI-assisted capabilities),
        and the upstream data sources required to fulfil a capability call
        (e.g. national company registries, sanctions data providers).
      </p>
      <p>
        Each recipient is bound by an appropriate contract (data processing
        agreement, standard contractual clauses, or independent-controller
        terms). The current named sub-processor list is below; we update
        this list when a vendor changes (last reviewed 30 April 2026).
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Current named sub-processors</strong>
      </p>
      <div class="table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Current named sub-processors">
        <table class="table">
          <caption class="visually-hidden">Current named sub-processors</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Vendor</th>
              <th scope="col">Purpose</th>
              <th scope="col">Region</th>
              <th scope="col">Data shared</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>Railway</td>
              <td>Application hosting + Postgres</td>
              <td>US East (Virginia)</td>
              <td>All customer + transaction data</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Stripe</td>
              <td>Wallet top-up payments (no Connect)</td>
              <td>US, EU regional processing</td>
              <td>Email, payment method (PCI scope)</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Coinbase x402 facilitator</td>
              <td>Pay-per-call USDC settlement on Base</td>
              <td>US</td>
              <td>Wallet address, settlement metadata</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Anthropic</td>
              <td>LLM inference for AI-assisted capabilities</td>
              <td>US</td>
              <td>Capability input + output for ai_generated / mixed transparency-tagged calls only</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Browserless.io</td>
              <td>Headless-browser rendering for scrape-tier capabilities</td>
              <td>EU</td>
              <td>URL + capability input for browserless-tagged calls only</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Dilisense</td>
              <td>Sanctions, PEP, and adverse-media screening database</td>
              <td>EU (Switzerland)</td>
              <td>Search names + identifiers for screening</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Serper.dev</td>
              <td>Adverse-media fallback (Google Search wrapper)</td>
              <td>US</td>
              <td>Entity name; only when Dilisense unavailable</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Resend</td>
              <td>Transactional email (signup, alerts)</td>
              <td>US, EU regional</td>
              <td>Account email address only</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Better Stack</td>
              <td>Log shipping + alerting</td>
              <td>EU</td>
              <td>Application logs (PII redacted at source)</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Anthropic Claude (Claude Code)</td>
              <td>Internal development tooling — not customer-facing</td>
              <td>US</td>
              <td>No customer data</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="small">
        Country-registry, vendor-API, and other upstream data sources for
        individual capabilities are documented per-call in the audit
        record's <code>data_source</code> and{" "}
        <code>provenance.upstream_vendor</code>{" "}
        fields. The sub-processor list above covers infrastructure and
        shared services; per-capability data sources may add additional
        processors documented in the call's audit body.
      </p>
      <p class="small">
        We notify customers via the contact email on file at least 30 days
        before adding or changing a sub-processor that processes personal
        data, except where the change is required for security or
        regulatory compliance and a shorter notice is necessary.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="transfers">
      <h2>5. International transfers</h2>
      <p>
        Our infrastructure and several recipients are located in the United
        States. Where personal data leaves the EEA or the UK, we rely on:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          European Commission Standard Contractual Clauses (Decision
          2021/914), with the UK International Data Transfer Addendum where
          applicable;
        </li>
        <li>
          the EU-US Data Privacy Framework and its UK Extension where the
          recipient is certified;
        </li>
        <li>
          supplementary measures including encryption in transit and at rest,
          access controls, and minimisation of data sent to recipients.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section id="retention">
      <h2>6. Retention</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Account data:</strong> lifetime of account plus seven years
          (Swedish accounting law).
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Audit records:</strong> {defaultPeriod} by default
          (Colorado AI Act SB&nbsp;24-205 minimum); configurable up to{" "}
          {maxPeriod} for customers with longer regulatory
          record-keeping obligations.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Customer-input:</strong> stored alongside the audit record under
          Art.&nbsp;30 (records of processing) for the same period as the audit
          itself; redacted in place when retention expires so the audit-chain
          integrity hash remains verifiable.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Trial-credit record:</strong> a SHA-256 hash of the email
          address a one-off trial credit was issued to, with the date and
          amount. Retained indefinitely and deliberately survives account
          closure — it is the only way to enforce one trial credit per address
          without storing the address, and deleting it would let the same
          address claim the credit again by closing and re-registering. Lawful
          basis: Art.&nbsp;6(1)(f). We do not present it as anonymised data: a
          hash of an email address is pseudonymous, because someone who already
          guesses the address can confirm it by hashing it. The account link and
          the IP hash on that record are cleared when the account closes; only
          the hash remains. To have it removed — accepting that the address can
          then claim trial credits again — email{" "}
          <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Server &amp; security logs:</strong> 90 days.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Marketing data:</strong> until consent is withdrawn or 24
          months of inactivity.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section id="security">
      <h2>7. Security</h2>
      <p>
        We use industry-standard measures appropriate to the risk: TLS in
        transit, encryption at rest, hashed API keys, role-based access
        controls, audit logging, and tamper-evident audit chains. Detail on
        our public security posture is on the{" "}
        <a href="/security">Security page</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="automated-decisions">
      <h2>7.5 Automated decisions and DPIAs</h2>
      <p>
        Strale operates as a <strong>processor</strong> for capability
        calls; the API customer is the GDPR <strong>controller</strong> for
        personal data they pass in. Under Art. 22, the right not to be
        subject to a decision based solely on automated processing runs
        against the controller. Strale (as processor) provides the
        infrastructure to support the controller's compliance — every audit
        response carries a{" "}
        <code>gdpr.art_22_classification</code>{" "}
        field (one of <code>data_lookup</code>,{" "}
        <code>screening_signal</code>, or{" "}
        <code>risk_synthesis</code>) plus a
        plain-language disclosure of what that classification means and a{" "}
        <code>dispute_endpoint</code> URL.
      </p>
      <p>
        For capabilities where the processing characteristics meet at
        least one Art. 35(3) trigger (Art. 9 / Art. 10 special-category
        data, automated decision support, or systematic monitoring), we
        publish a Data Protection Impact Assessment as a reference
        document. These describe Strale's own processing risk profile and
        mitigations, and serve as a starting point for the customer's
        own DPIA covering their pipeline:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <a href="https://github.com/strale-io/strale/blob/main/docs/dpia/sanctions-and-pep-check.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">sanctions-check + pep-check</a>{" "}
          — Art. 9 (PEP) + Art. 10 (sanctions) data via Dilisense (combined DPIA — both capabilities share the same vendor wrap and risk profile)
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="https://github.com/strale-io/strale/blob/main/docs/dpia/adverse-media-check.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">adverse-media-check</a>{" "}
          — Art. 9 / Art. 10 data via Dilisense (primary) + Serper.dev fallback
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="https://github.com/strale-io/strale/blob/main/docs/dpia/risk-narrative-generate.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">risk-narrative-generate</a>{" "}
          — Art. 22 automated decision-support synthesis via Anthropic Claude
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="https://github.com/strale-io/strale/blob/main/docs/dpia/company-enrich.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">company-enrich</a>{" "}
          — Web-scraped company profile via Browserless + Anthropic
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section id="rights">
      <h2>8. Your rights</h2>
      <p>
        Under the GDPR and UK GDPR you have the right to access, rectify,
        erase, restrict, port, and object to the processing of your personal
        data, and to withdraw consent where consent is the basis. To exercise
        any of these rights, email{" "}
        <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>.
        We will respond within one month.
      </p>
      <p>
        The Service exposes{" "}
        <code>DELETE /v1/auth/me</code>{" "}
        for full-account erasure (anonymises identifiers, burns the API
        key, zeroes the wallet, and clears the customer content from your
        transaction rows) and{" "}
        <code>DELETE /v1/transactions/&#123;id&#125;</code>{" "}
        for transaction-level erasure.
      </p>
      <p>
        A note on full-account erasure: everything you sent and everything
        derived from it is cleared from your transaction rows by the request
        itself — the request payload, the response, failure messages, the
        audit body, upstream source records and your idempotency keys. This
        is the same in-place redaction we already apply to every transaction
        once its retention window expires, so it is proven not to break the
        integrity chain: the chain hashes are untouched and every later
        transaction still verifies, with yours reported as redacted rather
        than broken.
      </p>
      <p>
        What survives is the processing record under Art.&nbsp;30 — that a
        call happened, when, what it cost, and its place in the hash chain —
        together with the{" "}
        <code>user_id</code> on that row, which points
        at your now-anonymised account row. A bare identifier is not a name,
        and severing it would break the chain every later transaction
        references. The one exception to the clearing is a transaction under
        a legal hold, which we are required to preserve intact; the erasure
        response reports how many, if any, were held back.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you believe we have not handled your data lawfully, you may lodge
        a complaint with the Swedish supervisory authority{" "}
        <a href="https://www.imy.se" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">IMY</a>, the UK{" "}
        <a href="https://ico.org.uk" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ICO</a>, or the supervisory authority in your country of residence.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="changes">
      <h2>9. Changes</h2>
      <p>
        We may update this policy. Material changes will be communicated by
        email at least 30 days before they take effect. The version date at
        the top of this page reflects the current effective date.
      </p>
    </section>
  </LegalDoc>
</Base>
